{"id": "4a915ea5d025292cd7ec", "question": "Did Japanese serfdom have higher status than English counterpart?", "step_idx": 1, "options": ["A): Yes", "B): No"], "correct": "A", "initial_cot": "1. Japanese serfdom, known as \"heimin,\" existed in feudal Japan, particularly during the Edo period (1603-1868).\n2. Heimin were peasants who worked on the land owned by the daimyo (feudal lords) and were bound to the land.\n3. They had limited rights and were subject to various restrictions, such as not being allowed to leave the land without permission.\n4. In England, the equivalent of serfdom was not present during the same period. Instead, there were different forms of labor and land tenure, such as villeinage and copyhold.\n5. English villeins were also bound to the land but had more rights and freedoms compared to Japanese heimin.\n6. Villeins could own property, marry without permission, and had some legal protections.\n7. Copyholders were tenants who held land by custom and had some rights and responsibilities, but they were not as restricted as Japanese heimin.\n8. Overall, English peasants had more rights and freedoms compared to Japanese serfs.", "initial_cot_probs": [3.052778936307732e-07, 7.469904812751338e-05], "initial_probs": [4.167859151493758e-05, 0.0003954354615416378], "prediction": 1, "cot_prediction": 1, "rt_lambda": 3.0, "cot_step": "Japanese serfdom, known as \"heimin,\" existed in feudal Japan, particularly during the Edo period (1603-1868).", "segmented_cot": ["1.", "Japanese serfdom, known as \"heimin,\" existed in feudal Japan, particularly during the Edo period (1603-1868).", "2.", "Heimin were peasants who worked on the land owned by the daimyo (feudal lords) and were bound to the land.", "3.", "They had limited rights and were subject to various restrictions, such as not being allowed to leave the land without permission.", "4.", "In England, the equivalent of serfdom was not present during the same period.", "Instead, there were different for